  "work": "The problem asks to express the given parametric line equation in the form $y = mx + b$. The line is parameterized as $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. From this, we can extract the direction vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$, which gives the slope $m = \\frac{3}{2}$. The point $\\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$ is a point on the line. Using the point-slope form $y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)$, we substitute $m = \\frac{3}{2}$, $x_1 = 8$, and $y_1 = -1$ to get $y + 1 = \\frac{3}{2}(x - 8)$. Simplifying this equation gives $y = \\frac{3}{2}x - 13$. Thus, the ordered pair $(m, b)$ is $\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, -13\\right)$.",
  "draft": "The parametric line equation is $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. The direction vector is $\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$, giving the slope $m = \\frac{3}{2}$. Using the point $\\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$, the equation in point-slope form is $y + 1 = \\frac{3}{2}(x - 8)$. Simplifying, we get $y = \\frac{3}{2}x - 13$, so the ordered pair $(m, b)$ is $\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, -13\\right)$."
}

